menu "CSI transmitter configuration"

    config CSI_TX_CHANNEL
        int "Channel to transmit on"
        range 1 177
        default 36
        help
            Must match CSI_RX_CHANNEL on the receiver board exactly. ESP-NOW
            refuses to send when the peer channel and the radio channel disagree
            (ESP_ERR_ESPNOW_CHAN).

            2.4 GHz: 1-14. 5 GHz: 36-64, 100-144, 149-177 in steps of 4.

    config CSI_TX_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Milliseconds between frames"
        range 5 5000
        default 20
        help
            20 ms gives 50 CSI records per second, which is a reasonable rate for
            watching a person move through the link. Go down to 10 ms for gesture
            work; below that the console becomes the bottleneck before the radio
            does.

    config CSI_TX_PAYLOAD_PAD
        int "Extra payload bytes"
        range 1 200
        default 24
        help
            CSI is estimated from the preamble, so payload length does not change
            what is measured. A little payload just makes the frames easier to
            spot in a sniffer trace. The minimum is 1 rather than 0 because a
            zero-length array member is a compiler extension, not standard C.

    config CSI_TX_PHY_HE20
        bool "Transmit as 802.11ax HE20 instead of legacy"
        default y
        help
            y - HE20 MCS0. The receiver gets HE-LTF CSI: 245 sub-carriers in a
                490 byte record. This is the C5 showing off what a Wi-Fi 6 radio
                measures, and it is the highest frequency resolution available.

            n - legacy 6 Mbps (11g on 2.4 GHz, 11a on 5 GHz). The receiver gets
                L-LTF CSI: 52 sub-carriers in a 106 byte record. Smaller, and
                directly comparable with CSI captured from older ESP32 chips.

endmenu
